WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.850
Elon Musk recently tweeted that he’s considering
creating a new university with a rude name.

00:00:05.850 --> 00:00:08.940
Texas Institute of Technology &amp; Science.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.040
This was most likely his idea of a joke.

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:16.160
Yet he’s been quite serious in his belief
that today’s university degrees are useless.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.599
You don't need college to learn stuff.

00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:21.859
Everything is available, basically, for free.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.359
He feels the main value of college is to test
a student’s resolve - will they finish their

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:27.970
annoying assignment on time?

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.239
Rather than: will they learn something from it?

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:35.850
And also, if you probably wanna hang around
a bunch of people your own age or a while

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.390
instead of going right into the workforce.

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:41.550
So I think colleges are basically for fun
and to prove you can do your chores but they’re

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:42.550
not for learning.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.680
Legendary Silicon Valley investor and PayPal
co-founder Peter Thiel thinks you should skip

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.320
college altogether.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.000
He offers a fellowship of $100,000 for college
students who drop out to start their own companies.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.780
So what do we do with universities, what do you mean?

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.520
We just try to get people out of them.

00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:02.240
Years ago, I had this fantasy of starting
a new university, which is sort of a better

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:06.789
version of an all-around good liberal arts
education with less political correctness,

00:01:06.789 --> 00:01:08.570
you know, less thought control.

00:01:08.570 --> 00:01:12.380
Thiel believes higher education is actually
a form of brainwashing.

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:16.119
There are still obvious perks of going to university.

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.829
Those with a bachelor’s degree still earn
$500 more a week than those who only graduated

00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:24.490
high school according to the U.S. Bureau of
Labor Statistics.

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:28.619
However, now there’s a plan that could topple
the Ivory Tower.

00:01:28.619 --> 00:01:31.990
Google made a huge announcement in the middle
of the pandemic.

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:37.020
It said it would beef up its online school,
offering new courses in data analytics, project

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.310
management, and User Experience (UX) design,
created and taught by Google employees in

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:42.900
the related fields.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.630
These courses are not meant to supplement
your university degree but replace it.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:51.210
Google’s senior vice president of public
affairs, Kent Walker, tweeted:

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:55.740
“In our own hiring, we will now treat these
new career certificates as the equivalent

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:58.740
of a four-year degree for related roles.”

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:04.350
Roles in high-growth, high-paying fields in
which Google says the average salary is $63,000

00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:05.350
a year.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:09.379
Walker wrote in a blog post that “College
degrees are out of reach for many Americans”

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.950
as they struggle to pay back student loans.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:19.260
Whereas Google’s six-month courses cost
$234 - the price of just university textbooks!

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.080
Tuition in the U.S. has ballooned over the
decades.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.040
The cost of tuition, fees, room and board
at a four-year private university in America

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.510
is around $52,000 per academic year.

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:34.689
The vast majority of college spending goes
toward paying staff and faculty according

00:02:34.689 --> 00:02:36.040
to the Atlantic.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.140
Despite some of the most expensive tuition
in the world, the U.S. lags far behind other

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:46.154
developed nations in math and technology skills
- more necessary than ever as our jobs 

00:02:46.154 --> 00:02:48.080
are increasingly automated.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.220
The OECD studied the competency of adults
around the world, surveying 245,000 people

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:54.450
in 39 countries.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:56.739
How did the U.S. fare?

00:02:56.739 --> 00:03:02.689
When it comes to solving problems with digital
technology, the U.S. was in 18th spot.

00:03:02.689 --> 00:03:06.620
America fared worse when it comes to math
skills - sitting in 27th place.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:11.720
This suggests a mismatch between what people
are currently taught at American schools and

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.890
what skills are actually required in the real
world.

00:03:14.890 --> 00:03:16.980
Tim Cook wants certainly feels that way.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:21.760
The Apple CEO thinks the one skill every kid
in the U.S. should learn before they graduate

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:23.760
high school is coding.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:28.829
Apple launched a program in 2016 designed
to help students from kindergarten to university

00:03:28.829 --> 00:03:33.329
learn to code and also launched free coding
courses for teachers so they can pass on their

00:03:33.329 --> 00:03:34.329
knowledge.

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:39.549
Cook pointed out that in 2018, half of Apple’s
American employees did not have a four-year

00:03:39.549 --> 00:03:40.650
degree.

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:45.439
His predecessor, Steve Jobs, famously dropped
out of Reed College after just one semester

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:49.540
which I talked about in the previous video
that I’ll link in the description.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:52.239
Jobs was one of many smart guys who dropped
out.

00:03:52.239 --> 00:03:57.030
Mark Zuckerberg left Harvard during his sophomore
year to work on Facebook full-time.

00:03:57.030 --> 00:03:59.860
Bill Gates quit Harvard to focus on Microsoft.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:04.249
Daniel Ek left his engineering studies in
Sweden after just eight weeks to co-found

00:04:04.249 --> 00:04:05.249
Spotify.

00:04:05.249 --> 00:04:09.319
Elon Musk has expressed his admiration for
people who've left school to start their own

00:04:09.319 --> 00:04:10.319
companies.

00:04:10.319 --> 00:04:12.909
“In fact, ideally you dropped out and did
something.”

00:04:12.909 --> 00:04:18.370
He is technically a dropout himself, leaving
his PhD program at Stanford after two days.

00:04:18.370 --> 00:04:23.530
He’s been vocal about not requiring a university
education to work for him, reaffirming it

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.639
when asked on Twitter.

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:29.180
However, it’s unclear how many people Tesla
has actually hired without a university degree.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.389
I asked them but they didn’t respond.

00:04:31.389 --> 00:04:36.930
Tesla is looking to fill 10,000 jobs through
2022 for its Gigafactory in Austin.

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.840
Some still require a degree.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.810
Others make no mention of one, including pretty
good engineering jobs.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:47.340
They do require the: “Ability to apply first
principles thinking to your problem-solving

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.340
approach.”

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:51.710
First-principles is the practice of questioning
everyday assumptions.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:56.210
For example, the assumption that batteries
for electric cars are expensive and that’s

00:04:56.210 --> 00:04:57.860
just the way it is.

00:04:57.860 --> 00:05:01.610
First-principles involve rethinking what you
know, which is what allowed Tesla to build

00:05:01.610 --> 00:05:03.180
cheaper battery packs.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:07.340
Musk applied first principles reasoning to
question how students should be taught when

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:11.639
he took his kids out of private school and
put them in a school he started in a corner

00:05:11.639 --> 00:05:13.580
of SpaceX’s factory.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:17.389
This is video from his Ad Astra school provided
to Newsthink.

00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:21.610
The school - which has since closed after
his boys graduated - focused on critical thinking

00:05:21.610 --> 00:05:24.370
skills and even had a class on rocketry.

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:29.449
So many super successful people believe there
are better ways to learn than in a traditional

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:34.110
classroom which brings me to the sponsor of
my video, Skillshare.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.199
Skillshare is an online learning community
